import sys
p = sys.argv[1]
lines = open(p, encoding='utf-8').read().split('\n')
idx = [i for i, l in enumerate(lines) if l.startswith('| 620 |')]
assert len(idx) == 1
lines.insert(idx[0] + 1, "| 621 | 2026-10-21 | **Keys and staff: all six sections redesigned from the learning goal, 24 lessons, no overlap.** Her call (2026-10-21): \"don't cling to the old ones, propose what is efficient for learning\", \"make them all, no overlap\". One engine (`KsEngine` + `KS_SPEC`, in `ks3.jsx`) gives every lesson the same three views: the whole picture, one at a time, her turn; a wrong answer says plainly it is wrong and shows the right one; keyboard first, staff where the idea is the staff. Sections and lessons: **1 Keyboard patterns** (kp-groups black keys in 2s and 3s, kp-repeat the 12-key pattern starts again on C, kp-middle middle C, kp-hear low and high by two sounds that get closer); **2 Note names** (nn-letters A to G and round again, nn-houses white keys by house position in her door/inside wording, nn-updown one white key right is the next letter, nn-find every key with the same name); **3 The staff and clefs** (sf-lines five lines four spaces counted from the bottom, sf-treble the Queen: high, right hand, curl round G on line 2, sf-bass the King: low, left hand, dots round F on line 4, sf-grand two staves two hands, sf-climb higher on the staff is higher on the piano); **4 Line and space names** (ws-treble E G B D F / FACE, ws-bass G B D F A / A C E G with her rhymes, ws-landmarks her five landmarks, ws-count name a note by counting steps from a landmark, ws-read from the note on the staff to its key); **5 Ledger lines and 8va** (ll-above, ll-below, ll-middle middle C written two ways, ll-8va an octave higher); **6 Pitch and range** (sr-size long is low short is high, sr-range the piano's range next to other instruments, from her RANGES data). Facts come from her material or the keyboard itself; the old per-tool lessons are no longer on Home (the tools stay in the Toolbox). Also: Home Keys and staff is one concept with the six sections folded (row 620), the open section is remembered when you come back from a lesson (`patch631`). Built by `patch630.py` (+ `ks3.jsx`) and `patch631.py` (`t635`). Opened all 23 new lessons at 390 px in English (all, one at a time, her turn, an answer) and the tap/key questions in English and Korean: no console errors. **Check with her:** the Korean lines are mine; claims to review: 12 keys = 7 white + 5 black per pattern, the Queen's G on line 2 and the King's F on line 4, treble C in space 3, bass C in space 2, middle C on the ledger line, 8va = one octave higher (8vb only in the old tool), long bars sound lower, A0 to C8 for the piano. |")
open(p, 'w', encoding='utf-8').write('\n'.join(lines)); print('ok')
